<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>

	<!--
		The melee affinity gene gets a blank xenogene background icon instead of Geist's own artwork.

		Split out of the convergence operation it shipped in, because comparing it against Geist 1.6 shows it is not an
		overlap fix at all. The operation replaces the gene's whole modExtensions node, but the EBSG skill-change block
		inside it is character for character what the gene already has. The single real difference is
		backgroundPathXenogenes, which goes from UI/GorkMork to UI/EXBlank. That is a cosmetic preference, so it sits
		here rather than with the two exclusion tags.

		Worth tightening later: replacing the whole node to change one path is fragile. If Geist ever adds a third mod
		extension, this silently deletes it. Pointing the xpath at
		.../modExtensions/li[@Class="VEF.Genes.GeneExtension"]/backgroundPathXenogenes would do the same job safely.
		Left as the original author wrote it.

		Verified against Geist 1.6: the gene has a modExtensions node, and EBSGFramework.EBSGExtension is still a real
		class in EBSG Framework, so the replacement value loads. Only the thought-side class was renamed, which is a
		separate fix made in the Beliar folder.
	-->
	<Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
		<operations>
			<li Class="PatchOperationReplace">
				<xpath>Defs/GeneDef[defName="GS_Melee_Affinity"]/modExtensions</xpath>
				<value>
					<modExtensions>
						<li Class="EBSGFramework.EBSGExtension">
							<skillChanges>
								<li>
									<skill>Melee</skill>
									<passionChange>2</passionChange>
								</li>
							</skillChanges>
						</li>
						<li MayRequire="OskarPotocki.VanillaFactionsExpanded.Core" Class="VEF.Genes.GeneExtension">
							<backgroundPathXenogenes>UI/EXBlank</backgroundPathXenogenes>
						</li>
					</modExtensions>
				</value>
			</li>
		</operations>
	</Operation>

</Patch>
